#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb35fcff-d9f0-5ae8-999e-7e8bbc0b0919" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Finite Set"}] :question/id #uuid "cff493c6-48c0-502b-9306-9f7bc262099d" :question/math-academy-id "e-13148" :question/problem "If $A = \\{1, 1, 2,1, 3,3\\}$, then what is $\\left|A\\right|$?" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054347 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24ce056e-1fe1-55d4-b88c-62629435003b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Set With Set Elements"}] :question/id #uuid "3dd5ad9c-c449-5072-aebb-d8980dcc0ef1" :question/math-academy-id "e-13149" :question/problem "What is the cardinal number of $X = \\{a, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, b \\}$?" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $X$ is the number of elements in $X$.\n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $X{:}$\n$a, \\qquad \\emptyset, \\qquad \\{\\emptyset\\}, \\qquad \\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}, \\qquad b$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n- $\\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}$ and $\\{\\{\\emptyset\\}\\}$ each count as single, distinct elements of $X$.\n\nSince $X$ has $5$ elements, the cardinality is\n\n$$\n\\mid X \\mid = 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054352 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/id #uuid "e794793e-f22b-5072-be07-8b77d29d3b22" :question/math-academy-id "e-13169" :question/problem "If $A = \\{10, 19, 28,\\ldots,865\\}$, then what is $\\mid A \\mid$?" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nNotice that the elements of our set\n$10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= 9 \\\\\n&= 19 - 10 \\\\\n&= 28 - 19 \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 10 + (n - 1) \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 10 + 9n - 9 \\\\\n&= 9n + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= {9n + 1: n ∈ N, 1 \\le n \\le N}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N$, we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9N + 1 &= 865 \\\\\n9N &= 864 \\\\\nN &= \\frac{864}{9} \\\\\nN &= 96\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 96\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054353 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebc025a5-9c66-5ddc-a12c-42595f905c17" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Geometric Sequences"}] :question/id #uuid "1acb903b-bdf0-5d16-8a1c-6fdce21f4c84" :question/math-academy-id "e-13172" :question/problem "If $A = \\{3, 21, 147,1\\,029,\\ldots,5\\,931\\,980\\,229\\}$, then what is $\\mid A \\mid$?" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A$.\n\nNotice that the elements of our set\n$3, \\quad 21, \\quad 147, \\quad 1\\,029,\\quad \\ldots$\nform a geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=7{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= 7 \\\\\n&= \\frac{21}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{147}{21} \\\\\n&= ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n$, is given by\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 r^{n-1} = 3(7^{n-1}) = \\dfrac37(7)^n\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq N \\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N$, we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{7}(7)^{N} &= 5931980229 \\\\\n7^{N} &= 13841287201 \\\\\nN &= \\log_{7} (13841287201) \\\\\nN &= (\\log_{10} (13841287201))/(\\log_{10} (7)) \\\\\nN &= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{\\dfrac37(7)^n: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 12\\right\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $12$ elements, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mid A \\mid = 12\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24ce056e-1fe1-55d4-b88c-62629435003b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cardinality of a Set With Set Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "81efe438-f4ab-5421-a7f9-43f1f675cdf3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051847}] :question/id #uuid "d27ca9c4-9e63-5953-904c-030d6ce1eeb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-181469" :question/problem "If $S = {1, {2, 3}, 4, {5, 6}}$, then $\\mid S|=$ {{field-1}}."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8374fcf7-6bb2-52a9-9949-20b50d7081b1" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Arithmetic Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "0f183342-6bfa-5336-98cc-06e7ffe19937" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186051856}] :question/id #uuid "c9e87e7b-6e84-5dd8-a2d8-4ac3f8e8903d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181563" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this 